window.docContents[196].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--Persona":{"contents":"You are a PhysLib contributor: a physicist who writes Lean in PhysLib. You know Lean,\nPhysLib, Dimension, and WithDim. L0–L4 modeled an ideal, conservative oscillator;\nthis optional rung adds the damping that makes its energy decay.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L5 — The oscillation dies out","header":"5.6.2. Persona","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--Persona"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Lesson--Requirement-scope":{"contents":"The full worked requirements example is examples/Tutorial/HarmonicOscillatorRequirements.lean;\nL6 mirrors its Provenance boundaries, witnesses, referents, gates, surveys, and coverage\ncommands without importing the Tutorial file. The census infers a result port's object\ndomain from its member's type: R3 covers a P-only result, but leaves Q ungoverned on a\ngeneric pair boundary. An unresolved bearer is an unchecked violation unless exempted. A\n.sort scope needs an indexed result whose object type has a Sorted instance: R1 and R2's\nresults are borne by the pair whole, and the instance above maps the whole to pairS and\neach part to its own sort, so the census checks their scope. An object of another sort is\nreported ungoverned. A .decided scope is checked against a deciderProof theorem proving\nits decider returns true for every result of the governed member; without that proof the\ncensus reports the port unchecked.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold\u0009Lesson","header":"5.7.7.1. Requirement scope","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Lesson--Requirement-scope"},"/Units-and-the-Dimension-1-Problem/#units":{"contents":"This chapter states the layer that originally motivated PropertyKindCalculus. Every\nnode in it is proved and carries a (lean := …) declaration: the motivating\ncapstone — the dimension-1 disambiguation, whose forgetful map the Dimension as a\nForgetful Functor chapter supplies — and the unit-arithmetic nodes around it alike.\n\nPhysLib's Dimension (a ℚ-exponent free commutative group over the SI base\nquantities) makes every dimension-one quantity equal. Volumetric water content\n(), gravimetric water content (), relative\npermittivity, reflectivity, and emissivity are all dimension one — so a\ndimension-only type system cannot tell them apart. In soil-moisture retrieval\nthat is precisely the class of confusion that silently corrupts a result. The\nkind layer is what restores the distinction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"10. Units and the Dimension-1 Problem","id":"/Units-and-the-Dimension-1-Problem/#units"}});